2026-11-19 20:15:04 - Agent_20261119201419 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed all 8 results from the "py/pydantic-dangerous-calls" query in the pydantic package.

Findings summary:
- pydantic/__init__.py:214 — dynamic import driven by a hardcoded `_dynamic_imports` dict lookup (only 'RootModel' -> '.root_model'); not attacker-controllable => FP.
- pydantic/_internal/_validators.py:87 (_import_string_logic) — `importlib.import_module(module_path)` where module_path derives from an `ImportString`/`ImportString[Type]` field value. This is by-design documented behavior, but if a model annotates an untrusted field as ImportString the value is attacker-controlled and reaches arbitrary module import (and `getattr` for arbitrary attribute). Marked MR since exploitability depends on downstream schema definition.
- pydantic/v1/parse.py:42 & pydantic/deprecated/parse.py:54 — `pickle.loads(bb)` guarded by an explicit `allow_pickle` flag defaulting to False; gated API, opt-in only => FP (fingerprinting of dangerous primitive in a library helper).
- pydantic/v1/utils.py:133 (import_string) — module path derived from dotted_path; used by `PyObject`/`ImportString`-equivalent validation (pydantic/v1/types.py:657). Same design-intent rationale as the v2 variant; MR pending confirmation of untrusted-model usage.
- pydantic/v1/utils.py:194 — `eval('__IPYTHON__')` with a constant string literal, no data flow => FP.
- pydantic/version.py:30 and pydantic/v1/version.py:25 — `import_module()` over a fixed hardcoded tuple/list of optional dependency names ('devtools', 'email-validator', etc.); no user input => FP.

No new independent vulnerabilities were discovered. All 8 locations assessed: 6 false positives and 2 requiring manual review (dynamic import path helpers that are only dangerous if a downstream model declares an untrusted string field as ImportString).
